<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jai Singh II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1688–1743</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Sawai Jai Singh II, Sawai Jai Singh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jai Singh II was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachhwaha</a> raja of Amber in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> from 1699 to 1743 and a leading noble of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>. In 1727 he founded the new capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a>, laid out on a grid plan. A keen astronomer, he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jantar-mantar">Jantar Mantar</a> observatories at Jaipur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujjain">Ujjain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> and had astronomical tables compiled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jai-singh-ii.jpg" alt="Jai Singh II" width="320" height="459" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artistUnknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1_Maharaja_Sawai_Jai_Singh_II_ca_1725_Jaipur._British_museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
